Durarara!! AU
Chapter 20 – Part 2
Curtis sat with the wind in his freed braids and the city spread beneath his heels like something that had finally run out of arguments worth answering. The mask hid the shape of his mouth. The sunglasses hid the worst of his eyes. None of it hid the exhaustion in the line of his shoulders.
“…So fuckin’ tired of everything.”
Then he threw himself off the ledge.
No buildup. No second glance. One sharp forward commitment and the high tower lost him to open air, gray hoodie and black braids dropping into the dark like a cut string. For a split second he was still readable as a falling body against the building’s light-spill.
Then he disappeared.
Not a clean landing in the visible street below. Not a body breaking across the next set of roofs in ordinary physics. One moment of freefall — and then empty air where the masked figure had been, as if the night itself had closed a hand around him and taken the rest out of sight.
From the access edge below the final rise, Celty’s whole frame locked.
“Curtis—!”
The name tore through the private channel raw and uncontrolled. Black smoke exploded under the yellow helmet as she lunged to the drop line, scanning hard for impact, for motion, for any remaining piece of the gray hoodie in the vertical dark. The street far below kept moving in ignorant streams of headlights. No crowd screamed. No obvious body marked the pavement. The absence was somehow worse than a visible fall.
He had been there.
He had said he was tired of everything.
He had gone over.
And then, in a way no ordinary jumper should have managed, he was simply gone — leaving Celty on the high tower’s edge with the wind, the dead phone trail, the memory of get out now, and the sick certainty that the man she had followed into the night had just chosen the drop again…
without leaving her a body to prove what the choice meant.
Across the ward, in a locked suite that should have still been empty of him, the air in the living room folded once — soft white smoke, brief as a held breath — and Curtis dropped out of it onto the couch like a man stepping off a curb.
He hit the cushions hard, long black braids spilling free of the cap and mask as the gray hoodie settled around him. For a second he only sat there with the residual night wind still in his clothes and the memory of open air under his heels. Then the sound left him, thin and cracked and completely wrong for anyone who had just watched the same man vanish off a tower.
“…Nothing…”
A short, broken beat.
“Ahahahahahaha—”
The laugh that followed was not joy. It was the ugly release of a body that had thrown itself into empty sky, folded through white smoke, and landed back in the same quiet flat with every problem still exactly where he left it. No death. No clean ending. No version of the drop that finished the sentence so fuckin’ tired of everything. Just the couch. The dark. The bottle still on the counter. The door that had closed on Celty. The phone at the bottom of the lake. Justice’s song still stuck under his ribs.
Curtis tipped his head back against the cushion and laughed until the sound thinned into breath, mismatched eyes wet at the edges from wind or something worse, mask tugged halfway down his face like even the disguise had given up mid-joke.
High above Ikebukuro, Celty was still scanning the dark for a body that was no longer falling.
In the suite, the man she was hunting sat alone on his couch and laughed at the nothing waiting for him on the other side of the jump.
Celty did not stay on the tower.
Once the empty air refused to give her a body, every other possibility became motion. Yellow helmet sealed, black smoke tight underneath, she took the descent hard and cut back across the ward with a courier’s ruthless efficiency — not the wandering search of someone hoping, the direct line of someone already calculating where a man like Curtis would reappear if the fall had not ended him.
The suite door was open.
That alone stopped her for half a second in the hallway. Open, not forced. Open like an invitation left carelessly behind, or like a man who had come home through a route that made locks optional. Celty stepped in at once, real voice already rising in the private channel—
and then the room answered before she could use it.
Curtis was on the couch.
Asleep.
Gray hoodie still on, braids loose, mask tugged down under his chin, sunglasses discarded somewhere in the landing. One arm hung off the cushion. The residual smell of night air and cheap earlier vodka clung to him. His chest rose and fell in the deep, heavy rhythm of a body that had finally stopped performing crisis long enough to shut down. No blood. No broken limbs. No rooftop ending written across him in any ordinary way. Just the same man who had ordered her out, climbed a tower, thrown himself into empty sky, and somehow made it back to the couch to sleep like the whole sequence had only been another exhausting errand.
Celty stood over him in the dim flat, black smoke pouring hard from the empty space beneath the lifted edge of the helmet’s seal, the private channel locked on a silence that was almost worse than shouting.
He was alive.
He was home.
He was sleeping with the door open as if none of the last hour required explanation.
For a long second the Black Rider only remained there, caught between the urge to shake him awake and the equally strong urge to sit down on the floor and stop shaking herself, while the open door behind her let the hallway light cut a pale line across the man who had disappeared off a tower and returned to ordinary unconsciousness like it was nothing.
Curtis did not open his eyes all the way.
One mismatched iris caught the hallway light under heavy lids, long black braids spilled across the couch cushion, the gray hoodie still wrinkled from the night air and the impossible return. His voice came out rough, low, almost ordinary in a way that made the last hour feel even more unreal.
“…I knew you never left.” A short, tired pause. “When I said get out, I meant go home.”
Not stay gone.
Not stop caring.
Not erase the morning’s door.
Just the blunt, poorly aimed command of a man who had needed the suite empty and had used the hardest available words to get it, then climbed a tower and thrown himself into the sky anyway.
Celty remained standing over the couch, yellow helmet now fully off again, black smoke rising in hard uneven ribbons from the empty space above her collar. For a second the private channel held only the static of someone recalibrating between rage, relief, and residual terror.
When her real voice came through, it was tight enough to crack.
“…Go home.”
A short, incredulous beat.
“You ordered me out of the flat, put on a mask, climbed a high tower, said you were tired of everything, and threw yourself into open air. I watched you disappear. I came back here not knowing if I would find a body, a void, or nothing at all — and you’re on the couch correcting my interpretation of get out like the main issue was etiquette.” The smoke dragged toward him. “I did not stay because I misunderstood a housemate request. I stayed because the last version of you I saw went over a ledge after a dead man’s suicide song and a drowned phone.”
Another harder line, quieter only by force.
“You want me to go home next time, say go home. Say I need the room empty. Say anything that does not sound like you are cutting the last living contact out of a night already pointed at the drop.” Her gloved hand flexed at her side. “And sit up. If you have enough composure to grade my compliance, you have enough to look at me while I decide whether I’m relieved you’re alive… or furious that alive still includes stunts like that.”
Curtis’s mouth curved in something too thin to be a smile. Long black braids shifted against the gray hoodie as he quoted her back at herself with ugly precision, eyes still half-lidded from the false sleep of a man who had only shut down because the alternative was continuing to burn.
“And leave Shinra out of this as a comparison for what I am doing in your doorway. I did not come here to be your replacement soft landing. I came because the line went dead after the day split open.”
A short, rough pause. He let the words hang, then drove the point home.
“So don’t be anything. I didn’t ask you to be anything. Go home to Shinra. Whatever this is… it’s not good for you.”
The suite went very quiet around the dismissal. Not the earlier barked get out now of a man cornered by his own answers. This was colder. More deliberate. A severing framed as concern, or concern sharpened into a severing — hard to tell which, and maybe that was the point.
Celty stood over the couch and took the hit without stepping back.
Black smoke rose in a dense, shaking column from the empty space above her collar. When her real voice came through the private channel, it was low, stripped, and dangerous with the effort of not breaking into pure reaction.
“…You do not get to quote me as a weapon and then rewrite the whole night into a favor you’re doing my future.”
A short pause.
“I told you I did not come here to be your replacement soft landing. That remains true. It does not become leave the man who just vanished off a tower alone with his empty rooms and call that healthy boundaries.” The smoke dragged toward him. “You don’t ask me to be anything, fine. I came anyway. I followed anyway. I opened your door on an empty fall and found you laughing yourself sick on this couch. That is already past the point where ‘go home to Shinra’ functions as a clean exit line.”
Another harder beat.
“Maybe this isn’t good for me. Maybe you’re right about that. I am standing in another man’s flat after being ordered out, after watching him jump, after learning his dead brother left him a suicide song, and I can feel the shape of my own life bending around the fact of you.” Her gloved hands flexed once at her sides. “But do not dress your self-erasure up as protection of my wellbeing. If you want me gone because you can’t stand being reached, say that. If you want me gone because attachment terrifies you more than the drop did, say that too. What you don’t get to do is throw Shinra’s name between us like a finished wall and pretend the only responsible woman in the room is the one who walks back to him without looking at the ledge you just used.”
The empty line of her neck remained fixed on him.
“I’m still here. That may be unwise. It is not confused. And if you keep trying to exile me for my own good, at least have the decency to admit it’s your fear doing the talking… not my safety.”
Curtis’s fists clenched against the couch cushion hard enough that the gray hoodie sleeves pulled tight at his wrists. Long black braids shifted as he pushed himself more upright, the residual night still in his clothes, the mask hanging loose under his chin like a discarded second face. When he looked at her, the tiredness was still there — but under it something sharper had finally been cornered into the open.
“…Okay, Celty.” A rough breath. “You wanted to see what’s abnormal about me, right? You want to know if I’m human?”
The question hit the suite like a line drawn on the floor.
Celty did not step back. Black smoke rose in a hard, attentive column from the empty space above her collar, the private channel going very still as she tracked the shift from deflection and exile into something closer to exposure. For a second she only remained over him, reading the clenched hands, the post-jump exhaustion, the dangerous willingness of a man who had already thrown himself off a tower once tonight and might now throw the truth the same way.
When her real voice came through, it was careful and unflinching at once.
“…I asked for honesty. Not a demonstration staged like a threat.”
A short pause.
“If what you’re about to show me is the real architecture under the scars, the recovery, the landing that should have broken the lot, and the way you vanished out of open air — then yes. I want to know. I have wanted to know since the door opened itself and you walked away from impacts that should have left more than attitude behind.” The smoke drifted toward him, tighter. “But do not do this as punishment. Do not do this as a way to scare me back to Shinra or prove that closeness was always impossible. If you show me, show me because the lie of ordinary has already failed… not because you’re trying to win an argument by becoming monstrous on command.”
Another quieter, firmer beat.
“I’m still here. That means I can stand the answer. Just make sure the version you give me is the truth, Curtis — not a weapon shaped like it.”
Curtis did not answer with another sentence.
He turned one clenched hand palm-up, and white smoke gathered there in a sudden clean surge — denser than vapor, sharper than breath, the same impossible substance that had opened his door without touch and erased him out of freefall. In a single pull it hardened into form: a scythe, pale and complete, edge gleaming with a cold interior light as if the weapon had been waiting under his skin for the moment ordinary denial finally ran out. Long black braids shifted against the gray hoodie as he held it across his lap on the couch, not swinging, not threatening — simply presenting the fact of it in the dim suite like a verdict.
Celty froze.
Black smoke exploded upward from the empty space above her collar so hard it nearly became shape on instinct, her own legend answering the sight before thought could catch up. The private channel opened on pure static for a full second. A scythe. Not steel. Not prop. White to her black. A mirror held up in the hands of the man who had just asked whether she wanted to know if he was human.
When her real voice finally came through, it was stripped raw of every carefully managed tone she had used all night.
“…That—”
She stopped. Started again.
“That is not a human answer.”
A short, shaken pause as the empty line of her neck remained locked on the white blade.
“I have spent years in this city as the only one who makes a weapon like that out of what I am. People call it urban legend when they are being polite and impossible when they are being honest.” The black smoke around her dragged toward the white scythe despite herself, like recognizing like. “And you sit there on a couch after jumping off a tower and form one in the opposite color, as if the most private piece of my nature just found a reflection in a man who keeps telling me to go home.”
Another harder beat, lower, almost unsteady.
“Curtis… what are you?”
Not accusation. Not fascination in Shinra’s clinical sense. The rarer, more dangerous question underneath both — the one that admitted the scythe had not only revealed him, but had changed the distance between them permanently.
Curtis looked at the white scythe resting across his lap, then at the black smoke rising from the empty space above Celty’s collar, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as something almost like apology entered a face too tired to perform it cleanly.
“…I’m afraid I’m not allowed to tell you.” A short pause. “Not because I won’t. But because I can’t.”
The distinction landed harder than a refusal would have.
Celty remained locked in place, black smoke thinning and then surging again as the private channel filled with the implication. Not secrecy as preference. Not the old Curtis habit of rationing truth until cornered. A hard limit. A sealed door that even the man holding a white scythe in her presence claimed he was unable to open.
When her real voice came through, it was low and sharply focused.
“…Can’t.”
A short beat.
“That is different from won’t. That is different from not ready. That is different from every other version of distance you have used tonight.” The smoke drifted toward the pale blade. “You can form that. You can jump off a tower and reappear on this couch. You can open a door with no hand. And still the name of what you are sits behind a lock even you say you don’t control.”
Another firmer line, quieter only by force.
“Who doesn’t allow you, Curtis?” Her gloved hands flexed at her sides. “Because if there is something on you strong enough to let you show me the weapon and still forbid the explanation, then this is no longer only about your grief or your pride. This is a binding. A brand. A rule with consequences for speaking.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, unyielding.
“I can accept a truth you are not ready to give. What I cannot accept is watching you drown phones, climb ledges, and order me home while carrying a restriction that makes even honesty itself partially illegal in your mouth. If you can’t tell me what you are… then tell me what happens if you try.”
Curtis turned his palm, and the white scythe dissolved back into smoke as cleanly as it had formed — one breath of pale vapor, then nothing. The suite looked ordinary again by force, which only made the absence of the weapon feel louder. Long black braids shifted against the gray hoodie as he let his empty hands rest on his knees.
“…I really can’t say anything. I’m forbidden from it.”
No theatrical emphasis. Just the hard ceiling of a rule he was not pretending to enjoy.
Celty watched the place where the scythe had been until even the last suggestion of white was gone. Black smoke rose in a slower, denser ribbon from the empty space above her collar, the private channel quiet while she recalibrated around a truth defined by its own sealed edge.
When her real voice came through, it was controlled and cold around the edges with understanding rather than surrender.
“…Forbidden.”
A short pause.
“Then we are not having a conversation about trust alone anymore. We are standing inside a restriction strong enough that you can show me the proof and still be unable to name the source.” The smoke drifted toward his empty hands. “I won’t demand a word your mouth is not allowed to release. That would be stupid, and it might be dangerous for you in ways I can’t see yet.”
Another firmer beat.
“But hear me clearly. A man who is forbidden from explaining himself does not get to use that silence as a free pass to throw himself off towers, drown every line of contact, and order the people who come looking for him back into safer rooms.” Her gloved hand cut once through the air. “If something has claimed the right to lock your name and nature, that makes you less alone in the cosmic sense and more endangered in the practical one. It does not make you self-sufficient.”
The empty line of her neck remained fixed on him.
“Keep your forbidden truth if you must. I won’t try to drag it out of you by force tonight. What I will not do is leave just because the explanation ends in a wall. You showed me enough to prove ordinary was always a lie. That means I stay in the consequences of the lie with you… whether or not you’re allowed to finish the sentence.”
Curtis’s mouth opened on the edge of another sentence — some attempt, however small, to push past the wall he had just named.
He never got the word out.
The prohibition hit like a hidden wire pulled tight from inside his chest. His whole body seized forward on the couch, long black braids swinging hard against the gray hoodie as a violent cough tore out of him and then broke into something worse. Blood hit the floor in a sudden dark spray. More followed, forced up from a depth no ordinary gag reflex could reach, as if the unspoken truth itself had turned into knives in his throat the moment he tried to give it air.
“Gyahhh—!”
The sound was raw, involuntary, stripped of every controlled performance he had used all night. Curtis braced one hand against the cushion and the other against his mouth, mismatched eyes wide with the shock of a rule proving itself in real time. The white scythe was gone. The explanations were gone. All that remained was the price of testing the forbid, bright and ugly across the floorboards between them.
Celty moved at once.
“Curtis—!”
Black smoke surged from the empty space above her collar as she closed the distance, no longer arguing, no longer parsing distance and doors. One gloved hand caught his shoulder, the other hovering near his chest as he choked through another violent heave. The private channel filled with pure urgent focus.
“Stop trying to speak it. Stop. You proved the wall — do not push it again.” Her voice was sharp with fear under the command. “Breathe. Head forward. Let it pass without forcing another word through whatever just punished you.”
She held him steady on the couch while the attack ran its course, black smoke coiling tight around them both, the open question of what he was suddenly less abstract and far more lethal than any scythe on his lap had managed to make it.
Curtis remained bent forward on the couch, one hand braced against his knee, the other loosely near his mouth. Long black braids hung around his face as each breath dragged in rough and shallow, the gray hoodie stained dark where the blood had caught him. The worst of the attack had passed, but the aftermath still shook through him in small, involuntary tremors.
“…I’m sorry.” Another heavy breath. “I can’t, Celty.”
Not defiance this time. Not a wall built out of pride. Just the residual truth of a man who had tried to force the forbidden sentence anyway and paid for it in blood on his own floorboards.
Celty kept her gloved hand on his shoulder until the breathing started to find a less desperate rhythm. Black smoke rose in lower, tighter ribbons from the empty space above her collar, the private channel stripped of argument and left with something closer to grim care.
“…I know.”
A short pause.
“I saw what ‘can’t’ means now. You don’t have to prove it again.” Her grip steadied rather than restrained. “No more testing the edge of that prohibition tonight. Not for my curiosity. Not for the argument. Not to make the honesty complete. Incomplete and alive is better than one more word that puts your blood on the floor.”
Another quieter beat as she glanced at the dark spray between them, then back to his lowered face.
“Sit back when you can. I’ll get water. And you’re not ordering me home while you’re still shaking from whatever rule just tried to tear the answer out of your throat.” The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, firm and unmovable. “Forbidden or not, you’re not alone in this room right now. That part I can still choose.”
Curtis’s complexion drained so fast it made the earlier blood loss look like only the opening act. Long black braids hung lank against the gray hoodie as the residual color left his face, mismatched eyes losing focus for a fraction of a second while some delayed internal consequence finally arrived hard enough to make his shoulders lock.
“…Ah.” A thin, almost embarrassed breath. “I forgot about this part.”
Celty’s hand tightened on his shoulder at once.
Black smoke spiked from the empty space above her collar as her real voice snapped through the private channel, sharp with fresh alarm.
“What part.”
Not a request for the forbidden name. Not a demand that he finish the sealed explanation. Just the immediate, practical terror of a man going pale after already vomiting blood from trying to speak around a binding.
“Curtis — what part did you forget. Is it pain, collapse, blackout, something worse.” She shifted closer on the couch, free hand hovering near his chest as if ready to catch him if the next second took his balance. “Do not be vague in the helpful way right now. If there is a second stage to what just punished you, I need the symptoms, not the lore.”
The empty line of her neck stayed locked on his whitening face.
“Look at me. Stay with me. If this is about to take you under, you tell me how to keep you here without forcing another forbidden word out of your mouth.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes grew heavy under the pale wash that had taken his face. Long black braids slipped forward as his weight began to lean despite the effort to stay upright, the gray hoodie bunching under Celty’s supporting hand. Whatever second stage he had forgotten was no longer approaching. It was here.
“…Just a little—”
The rest never made it out.
His body went slack against the couch in one complete drop, consciousness cut clean, the unfinished reassurance dying in his mouth as his head tipped toward her shoulder. No more clenched fists. No more careful answers. No more walls or weapons or commands to get out. Just the sudden dead weight of a man the prohibition had finished punishing for the night.
“Curtis—!”
Celty caught him before he could slide fully off the cushion, black smoke exploding upward from the empty space above her collar as she hauled his torso back into a safer position against the couch. The private channel filled with raw urgency.
“Curtis. Curtis—answer me.”
Nothing.
She checked the rise of his chest with one gloved hand, then the side of his neck, black smoke coiling tight and restless around them both. Breathing — present, shallow but steady. Pulse — there. Not the empty stillness of the tower drop. Not the final quiet she had feared when he vanished out of open air. Unconscious. Claimed by the aftermath of a rule he had tried to speak through anyway.
Celty remained half-kneeling over him in the dim suite, one arm bracing his shoulders, the yellow helmet forgotten on the table, and for a long second the Black Rider only held the foreigner who had ordered her out, shown her a white scythe, bled for a forbidden truth, and then left her alone with his sleeping body and a floor still marked by his blood.
“…You absolute idiot,” she whispered through the private channel, the anger thin over something much closer to fear. “You do not get to finish the night like this.”
She adjusted her grip, settled him more securely on the couch, and did not let go.
Moments later, color began returning to his face in slow degrees.
Celty felt it first under her supporting hand — warmth seeping back into skin that had gone alarmingly pale, the shallow breathing deepening, the dead-weight slack of unconsciousness loosening as if some internal system had simply finished rebooting. Long black braids shifted against the gray hoodie when Curtis’s head moved. Then his mismatched eyes blinked open, unfocused for half a second before the suite, the blood on the floor, and the headless woman braced over him all reasserted themselves.
He stared at the ceiling.
“…Well.” A rough swallow. “That fuckin’ sucked.”
Before Celty could fully answer, he was already pushing himself upright with the stubborn practicality of someone who treated near-collapse as an inconvenience rather than a stop. He extracted himself from her supporting arm, swayed once, then walked toward the bathroom with the gray hoodie hanging open and the residual night still clinging to him. Water ran. He washed his face hard, both hands braced on the sink, blood rinsed from the corner of his mouth and the worst of the roof-wind and panic scrubbed into the basin like it might go down the drain with the rest.
In the doorway behind him, Celty remained frozen for a beat longer, black smoke rising in a thin, appalled ribbon from the empty space above her collar.
Her real voice came through the private channel low and tightly controlled.
“…You passed out from a binding punishment hard enough to put blood on the floor, and your first act upon waking is to rate the experience and go wash your face.”
A short pause as she watched his reflection in the bathroom light.
“Do not come back out here performing recovery like that sequence was a mild system error. Sit down when you’re done. If your body is going to keep surviving things it has no business walking away from, the least you can do is stop treating the aftermath like spilled coffee.”
Curtis shut off the bathroom water and dragged a towel across his face, long black braids damp at the edges where the rinse had caught them. When he came back into view, the color was still returning, but the stubborn practicality had fully reasserted itself.
“No. It’s a punishment for telling you. Or rather — attempting.” He hung the towel and met the empty space above her collar without flinching. “So let’s drop that subject for now. ’Cause I’m very serious. I cannot.”
Celty’s black smoke tightened above her collar. Her real voice came through the private channel low and reluctant, but not pushing the sealed wall again.
“…Fine. For now. I won’t ask you to bleed for a name you’re forbidden to release.” A short pause. “That does not mean I’m pretending the prohibition isn’t sitting in the middle of this room like a third person. It just means I’m not forcing another attempt out of you tonight.”
Curtis’s shoulders eased a fraction. The next line came quieter, stripped of the earlier command.
“…I’m sorry for telling you to get out.”
Before she could answer that properly, he was already moving past her into the bedroom. A moment later she heard the computer boot — fans spinning up, screens waking, the rapid click of keys from a man reconstructing a drowned line out of cloud backups and contingency hardware. Celty remained in the living room with the blood still marking the floorboards and the yellow helmet on the table, black smoke drifting in uneasy slow ribbons while the private channel stayed open on unfinished reaction to the apology.
Then her PDA vibrated.
Curtis:
There. New phone with my old number and contacts.
Celty stared at the message for a second, then answered through the private channel rather than text, voice dry and tightly controlled as she looked toward the bedroom doorway.
“…You jump off a tower, vanish, return, vomit blood for trying to speak a forbidden truth, pass out, apologize for ejecting me, and then the next coherent project is restoring the phone line you drowned in a lake.”
A short pause.
“I am taking the restored number as proof you do not actually want every door sealed. Do not make me regret that interpretation.” Another firmer beat. “And come back out here when you’re done playing contingency technician. An apology sent from the next room after a night like this is not the same thing as finishing the conversation in person.”
Curtis came back out of the bedroom with a new phone in one hand, still sealed in that practical post-crisis calm that made the blood on the floor look like it belonged to a different night. Long black braids shifted against the gray hoodie as he held the device up briefly, screen already lit with the restored number and contact list.
“We have spares. Justice and I used to break our phones constantly.”
The explanation was almost casual. Almost fond in the way old survival habits could be fond — two boys destroying devices through fights, falls, water, and general refusal to treat electronics as fragile, then replacing them like spent tools. In the suite’s dim light, with the white scythe gone and the prohibition proven in blood, the ordinary logistics of spare phones landed with a strange kind of intimacy.
Celty watched him from the living room, black smoke rising in a slower ribbon from the empty space above her collar. When her real voice came through the private channel, it had lost some of the emergency edge and gained something more complicated.
“…Of course you did.”
A short pause.
“Two people living the kind of life that ends in white weapons, sealed truths, and tower drops would naturally treat phones as disposable.” The smoke drifted toward the new device in his hand. “I’m glad you restored the line. I’m less glad that ‘Justice and I used to break our phones constantly’ is the most normal sentence you’ve managed to complete without coughing up blood.”
Another quieter beat as her gaze moved from the phone to his face.
“Keep it on you this time. No lakes. No ledges as an excuse to go dark.” Her gloved hand flexed once at her side. “And sit down before you find the next practical task to hide inside. You apologized. You restored the number. Good. Now stay in the room long enough for both of those things to mean more than damage control.”
Celty stood.
The motion was clean, controlled, and almost entirely against the grain of what she actually wanted. Black smoke rose in a reluctant ribbon from the empty space above her collar as she reached for the yellow helmet on the table. The suite still held too much of the night — the blood on the floor, the restored phone, the apology, the empty place where a white scythe had been — and leaving in the middle of that felt less like boundaries and more like abandoning a scene before it had finished telling the truth.
Across the room, Curtis had already moved past conversation into practical repair. He brought a cloth and water to the floorboards and began cleaning the mess without ceremony, long black braids hanging forward as he worked in silence. No performance of recovery. No further invitation. No second order for her to go, and no request for her to stay either. Just a man erasing the visible evidence of a prohibition’s punishment while the deeper evidence remained in the room with both of them.
Celty watched him for a second longer than was wise.
Her real voice came through the private channel quieter than most of the night had allowed.
“…I’m leaving.”
A short pause, the honesty under it harder to seal than the helmet.
“Not because the answers are finished. Not because I’m satisfied. Because if I stay much longer after everything you showed me, everything you couldn’t say, and everything that put your blood on that floor, I am going to stop being able to pretend this is still only concern from a doorway.” The smoke drifted toward his bent figure. “You need the room. You said as much in worse words earlier. I’m trying to give it to you without turning it into another exile.”
She settled the yellow helmet under her arm, then added, lower:
“Keep the new phone on. If that binding hits you again, if the next ‘forgotten part’ takes you under, you do not get to clean it up alone in silence and call that strength.” Another firmer beat. “I don’t want to walk out right now. I’m doing it anyway. Don’t make the wrong lesson out of that.”
She turned toward the door, black smoke trailing behind her like a held argument, while Curtis continued wiping the floorboards clean and said almost nothing at all.
Curtis kept cleaning.
The cloth moved in steady, practical passes over the floorboards, erasing the worst of the blood as if the night could be restored by domestic effort alone. Under his breath he began to whistle — light, almost careless, the kind of thin tune a man used when his hands needed something ordinary to do. Long black braids hung forward against the gray hoodie. The sound might have been convincing from farther away. Up close, it failed. The pain sat too clearly in his mismatched eyes, unhidden by the casual noise, a quiet admission that the prohibition, the jump, the eviction, and the apology had all cost more than he intended to show her on the way out.
Celty paused at the threshold anyway.
Black smoke thinned under the rising edge of the yellow helmet as the private thought landed hard and unwanted.
Why does he do this to himself?
Not only the tower. Not only the lake phone. The whole architecture of it — surviving impacts that should stop him, carrying a forbidden truth that punished speech with blood, restoring contact only after destroying it, whistling through pain like the performance of okay might eventually become the real thing. She forced the helmet the rest of the way on, sealed the thought inside it, and stepped into the hallway before the reluctance could turn her back around.
In the stairwell her PDA lit up at once.
Two new courier requests. Overnight. Time-sensitive. The city offering her the familiar mercy of work when the alternative was standing outside a man’s door listening to him whistle through something that was not fine.
She stared at the jobs for a second, then started moving. Shooter would be waiting. The road would be simpler than the suite behind her.

Inside, once the door was shut and the floor mostly clean, Curtis sat back against the couch and opened the new phone.
The sync finished in a hard little cascade of restored data.
Missed calls.
Unread messages.
Anri’s name stacked in careful, increasingly strained fragments.
Rio’s lighter check-ins turning uncertain when the line never opened.
The screen filled with every soft attempt the drowned phone had erased from his immediate awareness — concern, confusion, the specific hurt of people reaching for a man who had decided, for a few hours, that being unreachable was safer than being seen whole.
Curtis stared at the list in silence, the whistle gone, the pain in his eyes no longer competing with any casual noise.
The new phone worked.
The old consequences were still waiting on it.
The new phone rang almost as soon as the message list finished loading.
Curtis stared at the screen like it had personally betrayed the concept of downtime. Long black braids shifted against the gray hoodie as he tipped his head back against the couch.
“…Already? I’m here.”
Then he saw the name.
Anri.
The sigh that left him was quiet and complicated — residual tower wind, residual blood, residual Celty at the door, and now the one person whose post-fight stare had sent him climbing in the first place. He answered before the second thoughts could win.
“…Hello?”
On the other end, Anri’s breath caught so hard it was audible through the line.
“Curtis-kun—” His name came out tight with immediate relief, then fractured under everything the silence had done to her afternoon and evening. “You finally answered. I called so many times. I texted. I thought something happened after you walked away. The fight, and then you just—there was no ring, nothing, and I kept thinking about the way you looked when you said you needed to be alone.”
A short, unsteady pause as she forced herself not to spill the whole frantic search into one sentence.
“…Are you okay?” Softer, but no less urgent. “Please just tell me you’re okay. I know you asked for space. I tried to give it to you. But not being able to reach you at all made it feel like space had turned into something else.”
Another thinner beat.
“I’m sorry for how I stared at you after the fight. I meant what I said earlier. Just… don’t disappear like that again without one answer. Please.”
Curtis looked down at the floorboards he had only just finished cleaning, long black braids falling forward against the gray hoodie as the residual night settled heavier in his chest. Anri’s worry was real. That almost made it worse. After the tower, the white scythe, the blood, and Celty at the door, he had almost nothing left that was soft enough to offer without becoming another open wound.
“…It’s alright, Anri. Just move on from it.” A short, flat pause. “I’ll see you at school tomorrow.”
He hung up.
No Anri-chan.
No I’m okay.
No extra sentence to hold the line open.
On the other end, the dead tone arrived like a second, quieter violence.
Anri remained frozen with the phone still raised, dark eyes wide in the empty stretch of evening street where she had finally gotten through and somehow lost more ground by doing it. The relief of hearing his voice lasted only long enough to make the cutoff worse. He had answered. He had dismissed the whole frantic reach in two thin lines. He had not told her whether he was hurt, whether he was angry, whether the defeated look from the sidewalk had hardened into something permanent.
Her heart twisted harder.
No Anri-chan.
That absence sat in her chest with ugly clarity. The affectionate little tag he used so easily when he was still willing to stand near her had been stripped out, replaced by a school-day placeholder and a hung-up line. In the silence after, the fear arranged itself fast and total: she had stared at him like a freak after the fight, chased him, corrected herself too late, and now the relationship — whatever fragile, unspoken shape it had been taking — felt fractured under her hands.
Anri lowered the phone against her bag strap and stood there as the city continued around a girl who had gotten exactly what she asked for in the most punishing version possible.
He had answered.
And in answering, made her afraid that reaching him had only confirmed the distance.
Anri nearly dropped the phone when it rang again.
Curtis’s name lit the screen so soon after the hung-up line that for a second she only stared at it, afraid the first call’s cold ending had been the real one and this was some delayed mistake. She answered on the second ring anyway, breath already unsteady.
“…Curtis-kun?”
His voice came through quieter than before, less sealed, the earlier flat dismissal cracked open by second thought.
“Hey, Anri-chan. I’m serious — move on from it. I’m okay. Just adjusting.” A short pause, rough around the edges. “I’m sorry for disappearing earlier. I really am….”
The Anri-chan landed first. Then I’m okay. Then the apology. Each one undid a different knot in her chest without fully erasing the mark it had left.
Anri’s eyes stung almost at once. She turned toward the darker edge of the sidewalk, one hand tight on the phone, the other clenched in the strap of her bag as if she needed something solid to keep from sounding as shaken as she felt.
“…Thank you,” she got out, voice thin with immediate relief. “For calling back. For saying that.” A short, shaky breath. “When you hung up so fast, and you didn’t say you were okay, and you didn’t… I thought I’d broken something. I thought the way I looked at you after the fight was still all you could see.”
Another softer beat, careful not to crowd him now that he had returned on his own.
“I can move on from chasing you through the streets. I can give you room to adjust. Just — please don’t disappear like that again without one line. Even a short one.” Her fingers tightened around the phone. “Hearing you say you’re okay matters more than you probably wanted it to tonight.”
A quieter final edge of honesty:
“…I’m glad you called back, Curtis-kun.”
Curtis’s voice stayed low on the line, long black braids shifting as he sat forward on the couch in the empty suite, new phone hard against his ear. The residual blood was gone from the floor. The residual truth of the night was not.
“…I’m sorry, Anri-chan. I’m not a regular guy who can be normal in society.” A short, rough pause. “I’m sorry if I also embarrassed you…”
The public fight sat under the words — the showman’s theater, the bucket, the crack in the man’s spine, the crowd turning him into a sudden street myth while Anri stood at the edge with her eyes closed. He could still see the way people had looked. He could still see the way she had looked.
Anri was quiet for a second, absorbing the apology without rushing to erase it.
When she answered, her voice was softer and more certain than the panic of the earlier unanswered hours.
“…You didn’t embarrass me.”
A short pause.
“You scared me. That part is true. The sound, the ease of it, the way the whole street started cheering like violence had become a performance with a moral ending.” Her fingers tightened around the phone. “But embarrassment isn’t the right word. I wasn’t ashamed to be standing near you. I was trying to hold together the version of you who sits across from me and listens… with the version who can end a fight like that in public.”
Another quieter beat.
“I don’t need you to be a regular guy who fits society cleanly. I never asked for that.” The honesty cost her a little, but she kept it. “I just need the person who protects people not to disappear afterward because he assumes the only thing left in other people’s eyes is the ugly part. You’re not normal. I can live with that better than I can live with you deciding in advance that I can’t.”
A softer final line:
“…So stop apologizing for existing the way you do. Apologize for vanishing if you have to. But not for being hard to categorize.”
Curtis leaned back against the couch, long black braids shifting against the gray hoodie as the practical solution formed out of the mess of the day. If disappearing was going to keep happening — towers, lakes, locked rooms, the old reflex of removing himself when the staring started — then the least incomplete version of care was making the destination findable.
“…You live alone, yeah?” A short pause. “Well, I’ll send my address if I ever disappear again. I usually just go home.”
The message came through a second later: a pinned location and unit number, plain and unadorned.
Anri stared at it.
For a moment the trust in the gesture competed with the pure geographic shock of the map. A few blocks. Only a few blocks. The boy who had walked out of a public fight, out of her correction, out of reach entirely, had been almost within ordinary walking distance the whole time she was spiraling through unanswered calls and worst-case versions of the silence.
Her breath left her in a thin, disbelieving sound.
“…Curtis-kun. You’re that close?”
The question escaped before she could make it sound less shaken. She forced the rest into something steadier, though the edge of emotion stayed under it.
“Thank you. For sending it. For trusting me with that after everything today.” A short pause as she locked the address into her contacts like it might vanish if she looked away. “I won’t show up uninvited. I understand space better than I showed earlier. But knowing you usually just go home… and that home is only a few blocks away… that helps more than you probably intended.”
Another quieter beat.
“If you disappear again, I’ll know where to aim the worry instead of throwing it at the whole city.” Her voice softened. “Get some rest. And… thank you for calling back. For all of this.”
On her end of the line, the map still glowed with the short, almost intimate distance between their lives — close enough to make the earlier panic feel newly absurd, and close enough to make the trust land heavier for it.
